</p>
<p>Let the speeds be <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> mph, and let <em>t</em> be the time in hours that it takes for the two trains to meet. At that time, A and B have traveled <em>at</em> and <em>bt</em> miles, respectively. After the trains pass, A will travel the remaining <em>bt</em> miles in 4 hours, and B will travel <em>at</em> miles in 9 hours. So <em>bt</em> = 4<em>a</em> and <em>at</em> = 9<em>b</em>. This means that <em>b</em> = 2<em>a</em>/3, and as we also know that 9<em>b</em> + 4<em>a</em> = 450, <em>a</em> = 45 and <em>b</em> = 30.</p>
<p>From the <a href="https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.242709/page/n23/mode/2up"><em>Third Penguin Problems Book</em></a>, 1946.</p>
